WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.660
- After more than three months

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.960
of hearing testimony and seeing evidence,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.880
a jury has begun deliberations
for the criminal fraud trial

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.250
of Theranos founder Elizabeth Holmes.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:11.250
Over the course of the high-profile trial,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.130
the prosecution set out to prove

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.410
that Holmes intentionally
misled investors and patients

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.770
about the efficacy of Theranos's
blood-testing technology.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.270
Jurors heard from 29 government witnesses

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.090
and reviewed reams of
documents, text messages,

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:27.150
emails, and reports.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.210
They also heard directly
from Holmes herself,

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.320
who spent seven days on the stand.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.850
She acknowledged regrets,

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.500
but maintained that she
never defrauded anyone.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.430
With cameras banned in the courtroom,

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.550
WSJ's Sara Randazzo brings
us all the highlights

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.053
from Holmes's testimony.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.290
Elizabeth Holmes shocked a lot of people

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.490
when she took the stand.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:49.410
What was the defense's
strategy in having her talk?

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.030
- She's good at working a room.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.380
She's good at convincing people.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.940
It's what she had done her whole career!

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.470
She had convinced tons
of high-profile investors

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.440
and Washington luminaries and the like,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.420
to buy into her vision, and really,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.140
this was just the ultimate sales pitch,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.660
her taking the stand to
tell her side of the story.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.100
- [Shelby] In the first
part of her testimony,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.320
the defense walked the jury

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.590
through the early days of Theranos.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.740
- They painted her as an entrepreneur

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:13.970
who had great intentions

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.880
and was really trying
to improve healthcare.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.040
She said, she "thought
things were going well,"

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.447
and she'd get reports
from a study saying that,

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:21.760
"Oh, these results were excellent,"

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.420
and she wanted to share
that with investors.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.730
And so she gave a lot of examples of times

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:27.770
when scientists and
others were telling her

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.350
things were on track.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.520
- [Shelby] Another big
topic throughout the trial,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.920
Holmes's comments about
her company's relationship

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.240
with the military.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.300
Here's a clip prosecutors played of Holmes

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.770
talking to investors in 2013.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.090
- We built the business
around partnerships

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.480
with pharmaceutical companies,

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.240
and our contracts with the military,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.470
wherein we could deploy our framework."

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.580
- A lot of investors said
that they backed Theranos

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.460
because of its relationship
with the military,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.420
which didn't exist.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.900
How did Holmes answer to that?

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.530
- She basically insisted
up, down, and sideways

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.760
that she did not recall
ever telling an investor

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.670
that the Theranos device
was being actively

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:08.810
used by the military.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.960
And so it was this very odd disconnect,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.037
where we had heard from
several investors who said,

00:02:13.037 --> 00:02:14.500
"She said this in pitches,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.800
and that was a big thing
that got us to invest."

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.160
- Holmes explained that
Theranos was jointly working

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.780
on several studies with the
U.S. Department of Defense,

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.370
but she acknowledged that
no military partnerships

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.420
ever resulted from the studies.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.780
Another key moment came
when Holmes conceded

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.360
to one of the prosecution's
key pieces of evidence.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.680
She admitted to altering
Theranos documents

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.170
to include the logos of major
pharmaceutical companies.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.630
Investors testified that
the doctored reports

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.650
helped convince them to
buy into the startup.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.990
- She said, "Yes, I added the logos

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.700
of pharmaceutical companies to the top,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.350
but I didn't think that was misleading.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.670
I just thought it showed that this report

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.510
was done in consultation with
the pharmaceutical companies."

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.630
So she didn't frame it in the
same way the prosecution did,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.730
even as she said she was
the one responsible for it.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.270
- [Shelby] Another key issue
in the trial was Theranos's use

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:06.390
of blood-testing devices
made by other companies.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:08.790
Prosecutors played this
video clip of Holmes

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.070
speaking with CNBC's Jim Cramer in 2015.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.720
- Every test that we
offer in our laboratory

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:17.530
can run on our proprietary devices.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.780
- [Shelby] Holmes falsely
claimed Theranos could run

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:23.200
more than 200 tests on
its proprietary device.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.740
In reality, the device could
only perform a dozen tests.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.200
- We heard her basically say
publicly for the first time,

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.010
what they did with these
third-party devices,

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.210
and that, yes, they bought
third-party machines

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:35.940
and modified them so that they could run

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.050
their tiny blood samples
that they said were being run

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.750
on their own proprietary machines.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.050
- A pivotal moment in
Holmes's testimony came

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.070
when she claimed she had suffered sexual

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.550
and emotional abuse.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.380
- It was, of course, very traumatic

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.500
and serious allegations that she brought,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.090
saying that her former
boyfriend, Sunny Balwani,

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.260
who was also for top deputy at Theranos,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.140
really controlled her
life on a micro level,

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.240
controlling when she could
sleep, when she could eat,

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.470
how she ran her business,

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:05.160
what she should do to be
a better businessperson,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.840
and even saying that he forced her

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.510
to have sex when she didn't want to.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.842
- [Shelby] Sunny Balwani has
denied the abuse allegations.

00:04:11.842 --> 00:04:13.440
Prosecutors presented evidence

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.480
in the form of hundreds of text messages

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.090
between Holmes and Balwani
showing loving exchanges.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:21.170
Balwani faces a separate trial next year

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:24.110
for the same fraud and
conspiracy charges as Holmes.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.290
He has denied the allegations against him.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.080
- It really was emotional,
powerful testimony.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.950
The problem was, it didn't relate

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.660
to the fraud that was being
alleged that had happened.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.570
She even explicitly said, "No,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.540
I'm not saying that Sunny
told me what to tell investors

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.260
and what to tell business partners."

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.930
But she was saying he clouded

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.860
kind of everything about what she did.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.800
- [Shelby] In the prosecution's
cross-examination,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.730
Holmes expressed remorse for how Theranos

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.410
treated lab employees and handled
situations with reporters.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.410
Prosecutors told jurors how Theranos

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.600
threatened scientists with legal action

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.150
to keep them from talking to journalists

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.830
about problems at the lab.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.660
Holmes also testified about her attempts

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.750
to quash "Wall Street Journal" reporting.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.710
The "Journal" broke story after story

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.110
about Theranos's failed technology.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.870
- I think the strategy there
is to make her look more human.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.270
If you take the stand and
say, "I did nothing wrong,

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:15.110
I've never done anything
wrong in my life,"

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.160
a juror's going to have a
hard time relating to that.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.527
But when she said things like,

00:05:18.527 --> 00:05:20.770
"The way we handled the
'Wall Street Journal' process

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.650
was a complete disaster,"
and, "Yes, I really wish

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:26.380
we had treated Erika Cheung,
one of the lab scientists,

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:29.290
better, you know, we could
have done that differently,"

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:32.190
it showed she was taking
some responsibility.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.850
- Holmes's final verdict
could have a lasting impact

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.960
in the world of Silicon Valley
venture capital startups.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.800
- White collar cases
are very hard to bring.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.660
Fraud is very hard to prove.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.860
And there's always a lot of questions

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:45.970
over whether it's worth
it for the government

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.140
to bring a case like this.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:49.220
And so this could give other companies

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.560
in Silicon Valley pause,

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:54.690
but it certainly is notable
in that it's very rare

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.430
for a startup and startup culture

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.508
to be considered a crime.

00:05:58.508 --> 00:06:01.758
(contemplative music)

